{"name":"Black Bamboo","hex":"#5b5d53","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"black bamboo","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Phyllostachys nigra, native to Hunan province in China and widely cultivated, used for lumber as well as in gardens."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/black_bamboo"}],"entries":[{"title":"Phyllostachys nigra","blocks":[{"text":"Phyllostachys nigra, commonly known as black bamboo or purple bamboo, is a species of bamboo, native to Hunan Province of China, and is widely cultivated elsewhere."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Bambouseraie_de_Prafrance_20100904_009.jpg/330px-Bambouseraie_de_Prafrance_20100904_009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phyllostachys_nigra"},{"title":"Bamboo","blocks":[{"text":"Bamboos are a diverse group of mostly evergreen perennial flowering plants making up the subfamily Bambusoideae of the grass family Poaceae. Giant bamboos are the largest members of the grass family, in the case of Dendrocalamus sinicus having individual stalks (culms) reaching a length of 46 meters (151 ft), up to 36 centimeters (14 in) in diameter and a weight of up to 450 kilograms (1,000 lb). It grows in tropical and subtropical regions, especially in Asia. The internodes of bamboos can also be of great length. Kinabaluchloa wrayi has internodes up to 2.5 meters (8 ft) in length, and Arthrostylidium schomburgkii has internodes up to 5 meters (16 ft) in length, exceeded in length only by papyrus. By contrast, the stalks of the tiny bamboo Raddiella vanessiae of the savannas of French Guiana measure only 10–20 millimeters (0.4–0.8 in) in length by about 2 millimeters (0.08 in) in width. The origin of the word \"bamboo\" is uncertain, but it most likely comes from the Dutch or Portuguese language, which originally borrowed it from Malay."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Bamboo_forest.jpg/330px-Bamboo_forest.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bamboo"},{"title":"Black","blocks":[{"text":"Black is a color that results from the absence or complete absorption of visible light. It is an achromatic color, without chroma, like white and grey. It is often used symbolically or figuratively to represent darkness. Black and white have often been used to describe opposites such as good and evil, the Dark Ages versus the Age of Enlightenment, and night versus day. Since the Middle Ages, black has been the symbolic color of solemnity and authority, and for this reason it is still commonly worn by judges and magistrates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png/330px-Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Big_Bamboo_Shoot_%28Joi_Ito%29.jpg/500px-Big_Bamboo_Shoot_%28Joi_Ito%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Big_Bamboo_Shoot_%28Joi_Ito%29.jpg/500px-Big_Bamboo_Shoot_%28Joi_Ito%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fresh bamboo shoot emerging from the soil. These young, tender culms of bamboo plants are also widely harvested for culinary use in Asia.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABig_Bamboo_Shoot_(Joi_Ito).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/2021_Sagano_Bamboo_forest_in_Arashiyama%2C_Kyoto%2C_Japan.jpg/500px-2021_Sagano_Bamboo_forest_in_Arashiyama%2C_Kyoto%2C_Japan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/2021_S